karadoc wrote:
>
>> Another problem I have with MC is that it is very hard for non-spoiled
>> players to figure it out. I'm not sure what a good solution for that
>> would be. Essentially MC is a parallel "armor class" system (to
>> "special" attacks, rather than regular ones), but the player has
>> absolutely no indication of what is going on, either in the status bar
>> or in the intrinsics list.
>
> I completely agree. I think it's pretty harsh that something as
> powerful as MC is completely hidden from the player. In fact, I think
> I'll add an MC display next to AC.

I think the change should be to make it more clear what happened. 
Instead of "He misses" it could go like "The vampire bites."  "His bite
is blocked by your cloak."  Seeing that it would become clear which
armor items are doing the blocking and how well.  It would be gradual to
learn but the data would be visible with that message change.

>> Another way to make the late game a bit harder is to remove unihorn's
>> ability to restore stats, a la Unnethack. That used to drive me crazy
>> at first, but the more I've played, the more sense it makes. It makes
>> potions and spellbooks of restore ability actually worth something.
>
> That's not a bad idea. I've always thought unicorns horns were
> extremely powerful, and potions/books of restore ability extremely
> worthless. I'll certainly consider doing that. Thanks.

I also like the idea of a unihorn having charges.  Thus making scrolls
of charging even more valuable than they are now.

> Interesting thoughts about resistances. I would like the other dragon
> scales (ie. non-grey/silver) to see a bit more action, and these kinds
> of idea to fit with my objection to being "immune to everything". But
> I'm not sure if making fire-res and cold-res mutual exclusive is the
> right way to do it.

Several of the dragon scales give resistances that can be acquired by
eating the dragon or eating other critters earlier.  This makes those
scales nice to the AC points but ?oEA are rare enough to not waste.  I
don't know if I find this frustrating or a matter of learning what to do
when.

>> I like learning immunities as a rite of passage. �There's a list of them
>> to be learned. �The list if finite and at some point you can fight
>> Rodney on an even keel for the Amulet. �Before then there is a list of
>> immunities to be learned.
>
> I think this is a decent way of looking at it. In addition to what I
> said above, I don't really want to spoil this 'rite of passage' feel.

Some folks find the resistances frustrating.  I don't since I started
seeing them this way.

>>�It's tuned so it is not
>> reasonable to be able to enter the Quest based only on kills short of
>> playing like you're an extinctionist. �That's a deliberate choice.
>
> But anyway, I don't understand why you think it's
> important that you can't enter the quest with only xp from kills.

Because the Quest is a qualification to complete the game so it makes
sense to require the player to have more of a complete set of skills. 
If a combat wombat can become enough of a killing machine to enter the
Quest and take out the nemesis in melee then it should take a vast
amount of preparation.  Using other means to level up shows a wider
range of skills and so the level requirement of the Quest rewards that
IMO.



